# The couplings, reduced to what an `association` can carry. One row per (filing, from_layer,
# to_layer): this filing observed that relieving one layer moves the other.
#
# The model's own falsifier, drawn
#   `pm:Coupling` exists so that the model can be refuted in its own format: a stack is assumed to
#   be independent layers, and a coupling is the observation that says it is not. A diagram silent
#   about it is the artifact a reader is most likely to be shown and the one unable to carry the
#   refutation, which is what this relation is here to prevent.
#
# It has a direction
#   That is checked rather than assumed. `composition/attenuated.sqlc` matches from to from and to
#   to to, `entries/spillovers.sqlc` joins each end separately, and
#   `checks/coupling_does_not_attenuate.sqlc` formats its own detail as `%s->%s`. Moving together is
#   a different relation: `checks/layers_move_together.sqlc` reads `rank/co_moving_layers.sqlc`, the
#   layers that reach each other through parts, so it is about the parts and never about the
#   couplings. Two rules, two graphs, one English phrase.
#
# Which layer, never how much, the observation included
#   Here that leaves out the `observation` as well as the strength. The observation is required
#   prose and it is the whole evidence, and some observations on the corpus contain a numeral: one
#   quotes 0.10, 0.22 and 0.35 outright. Carrying it into a `documentation` would put a figure on a
#   page that has no unit anywhere, which is the refusal `dataObject` carries, arriving through
#   prose instead of through a field.
#
# What the artifact cannot say
#   So the artifact says that two layers are coupled and cannot say what was seen. That is a
#   `loses`, and a wide one. It is still the only place the fact appears at all.
-- pm:Stack/pm:Coupling projected to its two ends; BPMN 2.0 tAssociation sourceRef/targetRef.
SELECT c.filing, c.from_layer, c.to_layer
FROM (
    :compose(entries/couplings.sqlc)
) c
